## 2. `include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp` — GGUF File Reader

**Responsibilities:** Parses GGUF v3 files via mmap, reads metadata KV pairs (all types including arrays), tensor directory, validates architecture (`qwen4exp`), provides `GgufFile` and `GgufModel` (multi-shard) classes, and enforces bounds-checked cursor reads.

**Proposed change:** The `GgufFile::parse()` method reads tensor names into a `std::set<std::string>` for duplicate detection, but the `GgufModel` constructor also builds a `std::map` index. For a 1,223-tensor file this is fine, but for the 24,576-expert native packs the per-shard `std::set` allocation is wasteful. Replace the `std::set` with a sorted-vector approach that avoids per-insertion heap allocation, and add a `reserve()` call.

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
@@ -1,6 +1,7 @@
         tensors_.reserve((size_t)n_tensors);
         // GGUF has no index to arbitrate between two tensors of one name: find() is first-match, so a
         // duplicate would silently win by position.  Refuse the file at open instead, naming both.
-        std::set<std::string> names;
+        std::vector<std::string> names;
+        names.reserve((size_t)n_tensors);
         for (uint64_t i = 0; i < n_tensors; ++i) {
             TensorInfo t;
             t.name = c.str();
-            if (!names.insert(t.name).second)
+            auto it = std::lower_bound(names.begin(), names.end(), t.name);
+            if (it != names.end() && *it == t.name)
                 throw std::runtime_error("GGUF: duplicate tensor name '" + t.name + "' in " + path_);
+            names.insert(it, t.name);
             const uint32_t nd = c.read<uint32_t>();
```

**Verification cases:**
1. Run `gguf_reader_test` (the existing synthetic GGUF test) and confirm it still detects duplicate tensor names and reports the correct name.
2. Open the real 4-shard Unsloth UD-Q4_K_XL model (111 GB) and confirm `GgufModel::open()` completes without error and `find("blk.11.ffn_down_exps.weight")` returns a valid pointer from shard 2.

